    "result_text": "Green. Here's what I implemented for the `riskFlag` feature.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `riskFlag` is `true` when any of the three signals hold, else `false` \u2014 surfaced on the owner response the acceptance test reads (`GET /api/owners/{id}`).\n\n**Three files touched, all changes small and cohesive:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `riskFlag` boolean to the `Owner` schema (next to `capacityWarning`), which regenerates `OwnerDto.setRiskFlag(...)`. No behavioural code.\n\n2. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new, self-contained helper) \u2014 a single static `of(owner, repo)` that ORs the three conditions, reusing the existing infrastructure so nothing is duplicated:\n   - **Possible duplicate** \u2192 `owner.getPossibleDuplicateOf() != null` (persisted by `DetectPossibleDuplicate`).\n   - **City over soft capacity** \u2192 `CityCapacity.approaching(...)` (the existing 40\u201349 soft-capacity window).\n   - **Disposable-adjacent email** \u2192 domain whose second-level label matches a known disposable provider (`mailinator`/`tempmail`/`guerrillamail`) under *any* suffix. The exact domains are already hard-rejected at creation by `NormaliseEmail`, so \"adjacent\" catches the look-alikes (e.g. `mailinator.net`) that slip through; a null/absent email is not a risk.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 one added line: `dto.setRiskFlag(RiskFlag.of(owner, ownerRepository));`, alongside the sibling `setCapacityWarning` call.\n\n**Structural impact:** the only *changed* function is `RespondWithOwner.service` \u2014 a one-method class (WMC_other = 0 \u2192 1), CC 1, 1 changed line \u2248 cost 1. All the new derivation lives in `RiskFlag`, a fresh zero-context class, so no existing method or class grew in complexity.\n\n`AcceptanceTest.coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1). The unique, email-less structured owner trips none of the three conditions, so `riskFlag` is `false` as asserted.",
